{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nstrategy = {\n  'status': 'ready',\n  'selected_prompt_family': 'paper_standalone',\n  'recommended_title': 'Missingness-Aware Latent Models for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction',\n  'central_question': 'How accurately can a historical-only, missingness-aware latent-model procedure predict an unseen embedding model’s recorded but unqueried benchmark scores at small query budgets, and how does this change under name-proxy-family-disjoint evaluation and destroyed historical cross-task alignment?',\n  'central_thesis': 'On the evaluated English MTEB panels, the frozen latent-model procedure has lower aggregate prediction error than the evaluated random-acquisition low-rank and greedy-coverage ridge pipelines, including on never-queryable audit targets, but the advantage is not uniform across held-out splits and does not isolate the contribution of mixtures or value-adaptive acquisition.',\n  'narrative_rationale': 'The strongest evidence is the completed final evaluation and its row/checkpoint records, not the search ordering or unverified internal diagnostics: these support direct error-versus-budget comparisons, common-target audits, an adverse family-disjoint split, and a historical-column permutation control. One shared question covers two split conditions on a single pinned embedding benchmark source; these are not independent datasets. The paper is an empirical method-integration study with explicit limits, not a new-architecture claim, a universal benchmark-compression result, or a campaign report. Final qualification is independently reported as completed/qualified by final.qualification.status and final.qualification.outcome; that administrative outcome is not evidence of universal validity, superiority, or mechanistic attribution.',\n  'prior_paper_story_status': 'rejected',\n  'edit_framing_decision': 'Not an edit-mode run: previous_paper, staged new/old programs, aggregated, extra_tasks, contributions.md, and instantiated edit-context files are absent. Rejected means no inherited manuscript story is adopted, not that an existing manuscript was evaluated and rejected. Preserve-the-prior-story is inapplicable; broadening the source brief to generative language models is unsupported; recentering on the latest quadrature branch is unsupported. Select the frozen evaluated latent-model family, with the two split conditions as evidence blocks under one thesis.',\n  'staged_input_impact': [\n    'No staged manuscript or edit-context payload was found; literal placeholder strings do not enable edit mode.',\n    'paper_data.json has no downstream_study and no study.* grounding sources. Do not invent campaign-selection statistics, original-incumbent comparisons, scored/unscored counts, or search-score improvements; do not substitute result.* sources for the requested study.* facts.',\n    'Private provenance only: results/best_program_manifest.json points to the latent-mixture source directory, not the quadrature extension. Describe the frozen evaluated procedure, including its Gaussian fallback; do not infer the actually chosen component count or selector from code availability.',\n    'The final evidence contains model-group and name-proxy-family-disjoint conditions, original/permuted historical-score arms, and a zero-weight reset replay. Those measured conditions set the scope, rather than the broader generative-model aspiration in the original brief.',\n    'ablation_studies is empty and candidate_diagnostics_trust is unverified. Static code explains available mechanisms but cannot establish which internal alternative ran or caused the measured advantage.'\n  ],\n  'method_groups': [\n    {\n      'name': 'Historical-only missingness-aware latent prediction and transfer selection',\n      'role': 'proposed',\n      'include_reason': 'The frozen studied procedure fits observed-data low-rank-plus-diagonal covariance, considers a small latent-mixture extension, conditions on revealed scores, and uses historical fixed-target validation with a Gaussian fallback. Present it as one validation-selected procedure, not separately measured component methods.'\n    },\n    {\n      'name': 'Random acquisition with masked low-rank prediction',\n      'role': 'baseline',\n      'include_reason': 'A genuinely executed comparison with explicit per-row/checkpoint records and final MAE; it changes both fitting and acquisition relative to the proposed procedure, so it is not an isolated acquisition ablation.'\n    },\n    {\n      'name': 'Greedy correlation-coverage acquisition with ridge prediction',\n      'role': 'baseline',\n      'include_reason': 'The stronger evaluated comparator uses overlap-shrunk historical correlations for coverage and ridge prediction. Retain its adverse-to-proposed family-disjoint split; it is a whole-pipeline comparator, not a matched predictor control.'\n    },\n    {\n      'name': 'Historical per-benchmark mean',\n      'role': 'baseline',\n      'include_reason': 'Provides a simple historical-only reference. Fixed-audit errors are available in observation records; primary remaining-target errors use the proposed policy’s residual target sets and must be described accordingly.'\n    },\n    {\n      'name': 'Historical-column permutation, queried-score average, and reset replay',\n      'role': 'supporting',\n      'include_reason': 'Permutation preserves recorded masks and column marginals while disrupting cross-task alignment; the queried-score-average control tests a deliberately weak score-copying alternative; replay checks deterministic reset behavior. These are distinct controls, not independent methods or evidence that any one proposed component is necessary. Keep the queried-score average and replay detail in the supplement.'\n    }\n  ],\n  'results_focus': [\n    'Lead with final-evaluation comparisons only: final.metric.candidate_mae=0.031296949105441715, final.metric.greedy_ridge_mae=0.036070484115358764, final.metric.random_lowrank_mae=0.04563401706946738, and final.metric.historical_mean_mae=0.08782454238980078. Report normalized MAE, lower is better; do not promote the search objective into a reader-facing metric.',\n    'Make the common-target view central: eight panel-level audit columns are never queryable, with at least four recorded audit cells per held-out row. Derive fixed-audit MAE by method, budget, split condition, and seed from final.evidence.episodes; retain the evaluator’s row versus family weighting. Descriptive all-condition audit means derived from recorded case summaries are 0.02687114697690909 for the proposed procedure, 0.029409541552239845 for greedy ridge, and 0.0354685223509185 for random low-rank. Label these as derived summaries, not extra runs.',\n    'Use k in {1,2,4,8} to answer the budget question without claiming a universally sufficient k or a threshold never specified in the artifacts. Show mean curves and all three frozen split-seed values, not invented significance intervals.',\n    'Expose heterogeneity in the main text and a six-row split-by-seed comparison table: in the family-disjoint seed 4021, proposed remaining-target MAE is 0.036658336620315146 versus greedy ridge 0.034144339636427614; also show its fixed-audit reversal using the recorded values. These are evidence of nonuniform comparative performance, not a diagnosed validity failure.',\n    'Original versus column-permuted historical data provides a dependence diagnostic, not a matched mixture/adaptivity ablation. Final reported proposed permuted MAE is 0.08976440453371944 versus original 0.031296949105441715. Use common-target records for the control figure; reserve the reported remaining-target permutation gap for a clearly defined descriptive table or text statement.',\n    'Qualification comes only from final.qualification.*: status completed, outcome qualified, correct true. Preserve this separate provenance statement and never infer it from search records. In any later audit/revision, retain a reported not_qualified outcome and all limitations if authoritative inputs change, without guessing the reason from metrics.',\n    'Do not include the Gaussian, mixture, median/mean, blind/adaptive, or quadrature branches as measured main-paper ablations: external paired component results and actual internal choices are unavailable. No original-incumbent final comparison is supported; the two directly evaluated native comparators remain valid.'\n  ],\n  'figure_plan': [\n    'Figure 1, early on page 1 or 2: one conceptual overview of a masked historical matrix, historical-only latent fitting and model choice, a query/condition loop, and prediction/scoring on unqueried recorded cells, with a visually distinct never-queryable audit boundary.',\n    'Figure 2, primary results: two-panel fixed-audit MAE versus query budget, one panel per split condition, comparing the frozen procedure, greedy ridge, and random low-rank; show the three split-seed values and their descriptive means.',\n    'Figure 3, dependence diagnostic: two-panel original-versus-column-permuted history comparison on the same fixed audit targets, one panel per split condition, for the same three pipelines; retain individual split-seed points.',\n    'Main tables, not extra figures: a four-method aggregate remaining-target/common-audit comparison and a six-row split-by-seed proposed-versus-ridge comparison that makes the adverse split visible. Complete checkpoint/control tables belong after references.'\n  ],\n  'figure_specs': [\n    {\n      'figure_id': 'problem_and_evidence_overview',\n      'required': True,\n      'purpose': 'Make the retrospective masked-matrix object, historical-only fitting, small-budget query/condition loop, Gaussian fallback, and never-queryable audit distinction instantly legible without suggesting a new neural architecture.',\n      'figure_type': 'diagram',\n      'preferred_generator': 'generate-concept-figure',\n      'group_by': 'scientific objects and information boundaries',\n      'metric': 'none; schematic with no invented score values or quantitative encoding',\n      'split_by': 'fit information versus query information versus withheld evaluation',\n      'preferred_span': 'full_width',\n      'target_width_fraction': 1.0,\n      'layout_rationale': 'paper.sty specifies one 5.5-inch text column and a 9-inch text height, not a two-column layout. Use the full 5.5-inch text width at about 2.1 inches high, roughly one quarter of a body page including caption, early on page 1 or 2. Four or five large visual units with at least 9-point final labels remain readable; a 0.48-width placement would make the information boundary text too small.',\n      'allow_progression': False,\n      'max_panels': 1,\n      'max_categories_per_panel': 6,\n      'max_series_per_panel': 1,\n      'max_panels_per_row': 1,\n      'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes'],\n      'source_paths': ['task/task_contract.json', 'task/evaluate_final.py', 'results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/program.py', 'results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/mixture.py'],\n      'export_requirements': 'Keep the editable draw.io source and vector PDF; export a 300-DPI PNG companion and reference PDF in LaTeX. If only raster is available, regenerate at at least 2400 pixels wide; never upscale.'\n    },\n    {\n      'figure_id': 'common_target_budget_comparison',\n      'required': True,\n      'purpose': 'Compare useful small-budget prediction on unchanged audit targets and show between-split variability without conflating removal of queried targets with predictive improvement.',\n      'figure_type': 'point_line',\n      'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib',\n      'group_by': 'acquisition and prediction pipeline',\n      'metric': 'fixed-audit normalized MAE, lower is better',\n      'split_by': 'model-group versus name-proxy-family-disjoint holdout',\n      'preferred_span': 'full_width',\n      'target_width_fraction': 1.0,\n      'layout_rationale': 'Use two side-by-side panels across the inspected single 5.5-inch text width, each about 2.5 inches wide, at a total height near 2.5 inches plus caption, about one third of a body page. Four discrete budget ticks and three method colors fit with 9-point labels and one shared legend. Do not shrink to a half-width float; simplify labels or stack panels if the final labels fall below 9 points.',\n      'allow_progression': False,\n      'max_panels': 2,\n      'max_categories_per_panel': 4,\n      'max_series_per_panel': 3,\n      'max_panels_per_row': 2,\n      'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes'],\n      'aggregation': 'Exclude replay and permuted arms. At each checkpoint aggregate observations.audit_mae or baselines[method].audit_mae across rows; model-group uses equal row weights, family-disjoint uses equal family means; then average equally over the three seeds. Plot the three aggregate seed values as unconnected small points in the method color and one descriptive mean curve, not as additional method series. The horizontal axis is evaluation budget, not campaign progress.',\n      'export_requirements': 'Export PDF and PNG from the same plotting source, PNG at 300 DPI; LaTeX must use PDF. Raster-only fallback must be regenerated at at least 2400 pixels wide.'\n    },\n    {\n      'figure_id': 'historical_alignment_control',\n      'required': True,\n      'purpose': 'Show whether the evaluated prediction pipelines rely on historical cross-task alignment while maintaining common held-out targets and identifying the control’s non-causal limitations.',\n      'figure_type': 'paired_dot',\n      'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib',\n      'group_by': 'proposed, greedy ridge, and random low-rank pipelines',\n      'metric': 'fixed-audit normalized MAE averaged equally over the recorded query budgets',\n      'split_by': 'model-group versus name-proxy-family-disjoint holdout; original versus column-permuted historical scores',\n      'preferred_span': 'full_width',\n      'target_width_fraction': 1.0,\n      'layout_rationale': 'Two side-by-side panels use the full single-column 5.5-inch text width, about 2.2 inches high plus caption, roughly one quarter to one third of a page. Three abbreviated method categories and two arm styles per panel leave room for 9-point labels, visible raw split-seed points, and a common y scale. No inset or third cramped panel is needed.',\n      'allow_progression': False,\n      'max_panels': 2,\n      'max_categories_per_panel': 3,\n      'max_series_per_panel': 2,\n      'max_panels_per_row': 2,\n      'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes'],\n      'aggregation': 'Use each non-replay case.audit_mae for the proposed pipeline and case.baseline_audit in documented order [random_lowrank, greedy_ridge]. These already average checkpoints with the appropriate row/family weighting. Pair original and permuted points within seed and condition; show all seeds and descriptive means. This refits/tunes the complete pipeline on permuted history and is not a pure covariance-only, mixture-only, or adaptivity-only ablation.',\n      'export_requirements': 'Export vector PDF plus 300-DPI PNG from plotting source and reference PDF in LaTeX; raster-only fallback at least 2400 pixels wide, regenerated rather than enlarged.'\n    }\n  ],\n  'supplementary_plan': {\n    'purpose': 'After references, provide complete checkpoint evidence, faithful implementation and data provenance, and control details needed for reproduction, while retaining the adverse split, common-target audit, absent component ablations, and qualification limitations in the main paper.',\n    'planned_subsections': [\n      {\n        'heading': 'Complete Budget-Resolved Prediction Errors',\n        'grounded_content': 'Tables for all recorded seeds, both split conditions, four budgets, proposed and native baseline errors, and historical-mean/queried-score-average controls, for both remaining-recorded-target and fixed-audit MAE. Separate original/permuted arms and omit replay from every performance aggregate. Generate these tables directly from final.evidence.episodes rather than rerunning experiments.',\n        'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.candidate_mae', 'final.metric.greedy_ridge_mae', 'final.metric.random_lowrank_mae', 'final.metric.historical_mean_mae', 'final.metric.observed_copy_mae']\n      },\n      {\n        'heading': 'Aggregation and Common-Target Comparisons',\n        'grounded_content': 'Document equal checkpoint weighting, equal model-row weighting for model-group, equal proxy-family means for family-disjoint, and equal case weighting over final seeds and conditions. Include seed-level paired deltas and fixed-audit versus policy-dependent remaining-target comparisons; distinguish descriptive split variability from independent-run confidence intervals.',\n        'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes'],\n        'source_paths': ['task/evaluate_final.py']\n      },\n      {\n        'heading': 'Observed-Data Inference and Historical Model Choice',\n        'grounded_content': 'Reproduce only the implemented observed-data factor EM, low-rank-plus-diagonal residual model, frozen-loadings latent-mixture option, conditional updates, bounded posterior median, covariance/square-root transfer heuristics, candidate hyperparameters, historical row-heldout fixed-target validation, and fallback. K=1 is the original Gaussian path, not evidence that multi-component inference ran. The implemented validation margin max(0.0001, 0.5 paired SE) is a selection heuristic, not a significance test. No measured component-ablation table exists.',\n        'source_ids': [],\n        'source_paths': ['results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/covariance.py', 'results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/mixture.py', 'results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/METHOD.md']\n      },\n      {\n        'heading': 'Frozen Data, Score Contexts, and Reproduction',\n        'grounded_content': 'Document the pinned MTEB revision, source JSON and resource checksums, full task/language/split/primary-metric/dataset-revision column keys, fixed metric transforms, natural missingness, content/identity separation, name-based family proxies, masks determining query availability, seeds, and exact final data plans. Provide the existing program interface and dependency files, and distinguish recorded evaluator timing from unmeasured method-only compute or cost savings. Explain final-evidence hash linkage; anonymize all identifying absolute paths in public material.',\n        'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.qualification.status', 'final.qualification.outcome'],\n        'source_paths': ['task/task_contract.json', 'task/resources/final/manifest.json', 'task/resources/final/dataset.json', 'task/requirements.txt', '/workspace/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json']\n      },\n      {\n        'heading': 'Permutation and Reset Controls',\n        'grounded_content': 'Show all recorded original/permuted control summaries and the zero-weight deterministic reset replay, documenting mask/marginal preservation and the difference between a protocol control and predictive evidence. Report qualification exactly as completed/qualified for these inputs; it does not certify unseen-model generalization or prove absence of every validity concern.',\n        'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.permuted_mae', 'final.metric.permutation_gap', 'final.qualification.status', 'final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.correct'],\n        'source_paths': ['task/evaluate_final.py']\n      },\n      {\n        'heading': 'LLM Usage',\n        'grounded_content': 'Disclose the documented roles of automated agents in idea generation, implementation, reference-query planning, and paper framing, and disclose later writing/figure assistance only if it actually occurs. Do not invent human verification, external reading, experiments, or a model identity absent from the logs.',\n        'source_ids': [],\n        'source_paths': ['global_memory/memory.jsonl', 'memory.md', 'reference_query_plan.md', 'paper_strategy.md']\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  'forbidden_headings': [\n    'Search Progression', 'Campaign Timeline', 'Milestones', 'First Valid Result', 'Phase One / Phase Two', 'Iteration History', 'Experiment A / Experiment B', 'From Gaussian to Mixture to Quadrature', 'Architecture Improvements', 'State-of-the-Art Benchmark Compression'\n  ],\n  'forbidden_phrases': [\n    'we then tried', 'subsequent improvements', 'first valid result', 'next-best', 'later variants', 'the winning idea', 'the latest variant proves', 'all families improve', 'eight benchmarks are sufficient', 'statistically significant improvement', 'the mixture caused the gain', 'quadrature improves prediction', 'calibrated uncertainty', 'state of the art', 'correctness rate', 'correct and eligible', 'eligible', 'combined_score', 'rubric', 'worker_01', 'worker_02'\n  ],\n  'notes_for_writer': [\n    'Use anonymous ICLR 2026 and the provided single-column paper.sty/paper.bst; paper.sty lines 48–49 specify a 9.0-inch text height and 5.5-inch text width. Full width and column width coincide here; never apply a two-column 0.48-width assumption. Omit names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, identifying URLs, and internal absolute paths from visible material.',\n    'Target an approximately 8.75-page body within the required 8–9 pages, excluding references; if the body is 8 pages its final page must be at least 75% full. Suggested inclusive allocations: title/abstract/introduction and overview 1.55 pages; related work and formulation 1.00; method 1.65; evaluation protocol 1.20; results including quantitative figures/tables 2.15; discussion, limitations, and reproducibility 0.95; conclusion 0.25. These allocations include floats and captions, not additional figure space. Finish with substantive interpretation rather than padding.',\n    'Thesis-first headings: Introduction; Related Work and Problem Formulation; Missingness-Aware Latent Prediction and Transfer Selection; Evaluation Protocol; Prediction Accuracy and Budget Trade-offs; Dependence on Historical Alignment; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Treat split conditions as two views of one question, not two unrelated experiments or a search sequence. Place Supplementary Information after references and put a separate LLM Usage section there.',\n    'Scope the object precisely: public English embedding-model results from MTEB revision 6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383, natural missingness, and 48 context-specific score columns per frozen panel. The final plans have 48 held-out rows and 88–99 historical rows; these are protocol facts in task resources, not newly measured performance. Do not claim all models/tasks in the upstream repository were evaluated.',\n    'Define primary error over recorded, unqueried target cells and audit error over recorded cells in fixed never-queryable columns. No missing entry is a ground-truth target. Explain why policy-specific remaining-target sets can favor different methods, and why the common-target audit is essential, not supplementary-only.',\n    'Preserve evaluator aggregation exactly: equal checkpoint means, row means in model-group, equal name-proxy-family means in family-disjoint, then equal weighting of original non-replay cases across seeds and conditions. baseline_audit is ordered [random_lowrank, greedy_ridge]; case.losses is [proposed, historical_mean, queried_score_average, random_lowrank, greedy_ridge]. Reconstruct final metric aggregates as a local consistency check before plotting.',\n    'Report observed between-split variation using the three final seeds 4001, 4009, and 4021. They may reuse models or families and are not independent population draws or pure optimization-seed replicates. Raw seed points and descriptive spread are supported; do not invent confidence intervals or inferential p-values. Do not turn the 48 rows or repeated checkpoints into independent repeats.',\n    'The family-disjoint seed 4021 is an essential adverse result; use exact values from the episode records rather than private memory or rounded copied text. Its lower ridge error is a performance comparison, not an identified validity/adaptivity failure. Discuss that family-disjoint averages need not worsen uniformly and the method is not uniformly best.',\n    'Explain the method as observed-data fitting and conditional transfer, not a newly trained embedding architecture. Training standardization uses recorded history only; Gaussian residual variance is task-specific model variation, not newly observed score noise. Finite latent mixtures and bounded medians are available options with a Gaussian fallback; actual selected K, rank, selector, fallback frequency, and mechanism-specific gains are not externally verified.',\n    'The selector excludes a queried task’s self-gain and uses cross-task covariance or a square-root-variance surrogate across other columns. The surrogate is not exact bounded-mixture expected absolute-risk acquisition. A constrained posterior median minimizes modeled absolute error, not necessarily real-data error. Gaussian unconstrained risk identities are explanatory mathematics only; do not transfer them unchanged to bounded actions or claim a new theorem.',\n    'Main comparison baselines are entire pipelines, so lower error does not isolate the covariance fit, mixture posterior, median, or adaptive acquisition. Internally computed validation diagnostics are unverified; no external mechanism ablations, uncertainty calibration, timing ablation, missingness sweep, cost-weighted acquisition, additional dataset replication, or theorem verification is available. Do not list absent experiments as completed or required main-story evidence.',\n    'Retrospective query availability is derived from each held-out row’s recorded mask, with audit columns removed. Make this information assumption explicit. Naturally absent scores are not evaluated, so the study cannot establish recovery under arbitrary missing-not-at-random mechanisms or prospective access to every benchmark. Costs are counted in queried benchmarks, not measured CPU dollars or equal real benchmark runtime.',\n    'Family labels are name-derived lineage proxies, and identity/content checks operate on the frozen resources. Do not turn them into a guarantee of biological/architectural independence, contamination absence, or population-wide new-family validity. Results concern pinned English embedding panels, not generative LLMs, multilingual transfer, ranking recovery, or downstream model quality.',\n    'Private-only provenance: the selected source is results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1, as identified in results/best_program_manifest.json; the quadrature extension is not the frozen studied method. No downstream_study or study.* sources are supplied, so omit search winner narratives, search-to-final gain arithmetic, original-incumbent performance comparisons, and scored/unscored attempt counts.',\n    'Private-only qualification provenance: final.qualification.status=completed, final.qualification.outcome=qualified, final.qualification.correct=true. This independently describes the final evaluation; it must not be inferred from search status or used as scientific evidence of superiority. If a later authoritative evaluation is not qualified, explicitly preserve that fact in writing, review, audit, and revisions; unknown gate diagnostics stay unknown.',\n    'At inspection, references.bib and references_provenance.json exist and agree on a retrieved method-oriented pool, but essential MTEB and several task-neighborhood starter titles are not present. Metadata retrieval is not evidence that a paper’s findings were read. Prefer a small relevant subset only after permitted original-source verification; safely obtain missing explicitly allowed starter sources before final drafting, without broad domain discovery or citation chasing. Do not use irrelevant retrieved references as padding or claim novelty priority from this incomplete pool. This stage performs no external retrieval.',\n    'Use final.* source IDs for measured claims. Supplementary entries with empty source_ids are implementation or process exposition grounded by their explicit source_paths, not invented quantitative source IDs. Treat final.evidence.episodes as the parent source for derived condition/checkpoint tables and record exact JSON pointers and aggregation code in downstream claim/figure mapping.',\n    'Plan all quantitative figures as source-generated vector PDF plus 300-DPI PNG, and use PDF from LaTeX when available. Full-width raster-only figures require at least 2400 pixels; a genuinely narrower column-width raster in a different format requires at least 1200 pixels. Never upscale; regenerate from source. Record final placement, width fraction, and minimum rendered font in the downstream figure manifest.',\n    'Only the early overview uses generate-concept-figure; keep its editable draw.io source and deterministic content plan. No fabricated heatmap scores, architecture blocks, or effect-size arrows. Required figures have at most two panels per row, at most six categories, and at most three quantitative method series; labels should remain at least 9 points at final size.',\n    'Do not draft paper.tex or claims_grounding.json, generate figures, run the evaluator, or run final-paper validation in this framing stage. A local JSON/source-ID/figure-budget consistency check of the strategy is permitted and distinct from final-paper validation.'\n  ],\n  'failure_reason': ''\n}\n\n# Preserve exact grounded values in the adverse-result instruction.\npaper_data = json.loads(Path('paper_data.json').read_text())\nadverse_episode = next(episode for episode in paper_data['final_evaluation']['evaluation_evidence']['episodes'] if episode['seed'] == 4021)\nadverse_case = next(case for case in adverse_episode['solution']['cases'] if case['case'] == 'family_disjoint' and case['arm'] == 'original' and not case['replay'])\nstrategy['results_focus'][3] = (\n  'Expose heterogeneity in the main text and a six-row split-by-seed comparison table: in the family-disjoint seed 4021, '\n  f\\\"proposed remaining-target MAE is {adverse_case['losses'][0]} versus greedy ridge {adverse_case['losses'][4]}; \\\"\n  f\\\"fixed-audit MAE is {adverse_case['audit_mae']} versus greedy ridge {adverse_case['baseline_audit'][1]}. \\\"\n  'These are evidence of nonuniform comparative performance, not a diagnosed validity failure.'\n)\nPath('paper_strategy.json').write_text(json.dumps(strategy, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + '\\\\n')\n\nmemo = '''# Paper Framing Strategy\n\n## Decision and Thesis\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** \"'`paper_standalone`, compatible with `write-paper-standalone`. This stage plans a paper; it does not draft one.\n\n**Provisional title:** Missingness-Aware Latent Models for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction.\n\n**Central question:** How accurately can a historical-only, missingness-aware latent-model procedure predict an unseen embedding model’s recorded but unqueried benchmark scores at small query budgets, and how does this change under name-proxy-family-disjoint evaluation and destroyed historical cross-task alignment?\n\n**Central thesis:** On the evaluated English MTEB panels, the frozen latent-model procedure has lower aggregate prediction error than the evaluated random-acquisition low-rank and greedy-coverage ridge pipelines, including on never-queryable audit targets, but the advantage is not uniform across held-out splits and does not isolate the contribution of mixtures or value-adaptive acquisition.\n\nThis is one standalone empirical method-integration study, organized around prediction under a query budget. The two holdout conditions are evidence blocks under that question, not independent datasets or a sequence of discoveries. The observed advantage is descriptive; it is neither a priority claim nor an assertion that a new neural architecture was developed.\n\n## Evidence Authority and Qualification\n\n`paper_data.json.final_evaluation` is read independently of any downstream-study narrative. The current input has no `downstream_study` and no `study.*` grounding IDs. Therefore omit campaign winner narratives, search-score deltas, scored/unscored attempt counts, and original-incumbent performance claims. In any future input, unscored attempts remain missing evidence, not measured losses.\n\nThe current final evaluation is **completed and qualified**, grounded specifically in `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, and `final.qualification.correct`. This is separate from search selection. Qualification is not a scientific guarantee of generalization, mechanistic validity, or superiority. If authoritative qualification changes during later review or revision, preserve its reported outcome—including failure—and limitations rather than explaining it away using retained metrics. Missing gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n\nThe final episode evidence is available and its local file hash matches the recorded SHA-256. Its three seed records contain original and permuted-history cases for model-group and name-proxy-family-disjoint holdouts, plus a zero-weight reset replay. Exclude replay from performance averages. Candidate-generated internal diagnostics are explicitly unverified. `ablation_studies` is empty.\n\nSource precedence is: final metric and qualification IDs for headline facts; `final.evidence.episodes` for recorded row/checkpoint/control results; frozen task resources and the selected implementation for protocol and method exposition; reference provenance for bibliography identity, not unread scientific findings. Private memory is not quantitative evidence.\n\n## Framing Alternatives and Edit Decision\n\nNo `previous_paper/`, staged new/old program pair, `aggregated/`, `extra_tasks/`, contributions scaffold, or instantiated edit-context file is present. Literal placeholders do not activate edit mode. The JSON status `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` means no inherited narrative is adopted, not that a prior manuscript was scientifically rejected.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve an existing manuscript with local updates | Inapplicable: no seeded manuscript is supplied. Do not invent its title or thesis. |\n| Preserve the broad generative-LLM aspiration from the source brief | Reject: the measured data are English embedding-model results, not generative-LLM benchmarks. |\n| Broaden to a shared latent-model family under two holdout conditions | Select, narrowly: explain covariance fitting, optional mixture inference, conditional prediction, and historical model choice as one frozen procedure, evaluated on one benchmark source. |\n| Recenter on the newest quadrature acquisition branch | Reject: static availability and search ties do not make it the frozen evaluated method or establish an acquisition benefit. |\n| Lead with uniform superiority or a mixture-specific discovery | Reject: a family-disjoint split favors ridge, actual internal choices are unverified, and external component ablations are missing. |\n\nPrivate provenance: `results/best_program_manifest.json` identifies `results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1`. Use its implemented procedure and fallback, not a chronology of candidate branches. Do not expose internal directory or selection terminology in the paper.\n\n## Scientific Scope and Included Methods\n\nThe data are pinned MTEB embedding results at revision `6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383`. Each frozen panel has 48 context-specific columns keyed by task, language, split, primary metric, and dataset revision, with natural missingness. Final plans contain 48 held-out rows and 88–99 historical rows. Budgets are `k={1,2,4,8}`; eight panel columns are never queryable, and each eligible held-out row has at least four recorded audit cells. These are task-resource protocol facts, not new performance measurements or full-repository coverage claims.\n\n1. **Proposed procedure:** observed-data low-rank-plus-diagonal covariance fitting; optional small latent-mixture prior; conditional score prediction and transfer selection; historical row-heldout fixed-target model choice; Gaussian fallback. The available single-component path must remain explicit. Code availability does not show the selected component count or selector.\n2. **Random acquisition plus masked low-rank prediction:** retain the genuinely executed baseline. It differs in acquisition and fitting, so it does not isolate acquisition quality.\n3. **Greedy correlation coverage plus ridge prediction:** retain the stronger whole-pipeline comparator, including cases where it wins.\n4. **Historical per-benchmark mean:** retain as a simple historical reference, clarifying the target-set convention.\n5. **Supporting controls:** column-permuted history in the main analysis; queried-score-average and reset replay details in the supplement. Neither protocol completion nor a weak score-copying control is a performance contribution.\n\nDo not add measured method groups for Gaussian versus mixture, mean versus median, adaptive versus blind acquisition, or quadrature. The source implements some internal comparisons, but their results are not externally verified. Do not infer an original-starter final comparison where no such evaluated comparison is supplied.\n\nExplain observed-data fitting, not a zero-filled score likelihood. Standardization uses recorded historical entries only; residual variance denotes task-specific variation. The constrained posterior median is optimal for modeled absolute loss, not a proof of real-data optimality. The square-root transfer criterion is a heuristic; it is not exact bounded-mixture integrated absolute risk. Gaussian unconstrained-risk identities cannot be reused unchanged for bounded actions. No new theorem or experimental theory verification is established.\n\n## Results That Carry the Paper\n\n### Aggregate accuracy and unchanged-target accuracy\n\nThe following primary final metrics are authoritative:\n\n| Evaluated pipeline | Remaining-recorded-target normalized MAE |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Frozen proposed procedure | 0.031296949105441715 |\n| Greedy coverage plus ridge | 0.036070484115358764 |\n| Random acquisition plus low-rank | 0.04563401706946738 |\n| Historical mean | 0.08782454238980078 |\n\nSources: corresponding `final.metric.*` IDs. Reader-facing presentation should round consistently and label lower as better. Do not display an internal search objective.\n\nRemaining-target sets depend on which tasks each method queries. Consequently the common-target audit is essential to the central thesis, not an appendix-only reassurance. Audit columns are never selectable and only their recorded cells are scored. Descriptive audit means derived from the six non-replay original case summaries are 0.02687114697690909 for the proposed procedure, 0.029409541552239845 for ridge, and 0.0354685223509185 for random low-rank. These are deterministic summaries of existing evidence, not additional experiments. Record JSON pointers and aggregation code in downstream figure/claim mapping.\n\n### Budget trade-offs and the adverse split\n\nUse fixed-audit error at each recorded budget, broken out by holdout condition. Show all three split-seed values and their descriptive means; this answers how prediction changes within the tested budget range without inventing a universal sufficient budget or an acceptable-error threshold.\n\nInclude a main-text six-row table for the two conditions and three seeds. In particular, retain the family-disjoint seed 4021 reversal: proposed remaining-target MAE is **ADVERSE_PRIMARY**, compared with ridge **ADVERSE_RIDGE**; audit MAE is **ADVERSE_AUDIT**, compared with ridge **ADVERSE_RIDGE_AUDIT**. These are comparative performance results, not a diagnosed validity or adaptivity failure. Neither a favorable aggregate nor administrative qualification may erase this observation.\n\n### Dependence on historical alignment\n\nThe reported proposed MAE changes from 0.031296949105441715 with original history to 0.08976440453371944 under historical-column permutation (`final.metric.permuted_mae`). For the figure, prefer unchanged audit targets and show all three evaluated pipelines in both holdout conditions. The transformation preserves recorded masks and column marginals while disrupting cross-task alignment. Because the entire pipeline is refitted and retuned, this is not a pure component ablation or a proof that a particular representation captures true latent skills.\n\n### Aggregation and uncertainty\n\nFollow `task/evaluate_final.py` exactly: average checkpoints equally; use equal row weights for model-group; use equal proxy-family means for family-disjoint; then average equally over original non-replay cases across conditions and seeds. Case loss order is proposed, historical mean, queried-score average, random low-rank, greedy ridge. Audit baseline order is random low-rank, greedy ridge. Do not accidentally reverse them.\n\nSeeds 4001, 4009, and 4021 specify frozen split realizations that may reuse rows or families; they are not independent population draws or pure optimization-seed repeats. Show raw split values and descriptive variation, not fabricated confidence intervals, significance tests, or pseudo-replication over rows/checkpoints. Historical internal paired-SE selection rules are not inferential evidence about final superiority.\n\n## Figures, Geometry, and Footprints\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` sets **one 5.5-inch text column and a 9.0-inch text height** (lines 48–49). Thus full text width and column width coincide. A two-column 0.48-width assumption would be incorrect. All required figures target the full 5.5-inch text width with labels at least 9 points after placement. Do not rescue dense panels by shrinking them.\n\n| Figure | Contents and generator | Readability budget | Placement and intended footprint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1: Problem and evidence overview | `diagram` via `generate-concept-figure`: masked historical matrix; historical-only fitting/model choice with Gaussian fallback; query/condition loop; unqueried predictions; fixed audit boundary. No invented numeric cells. | One panel, at most six major objects, one schematic series; four or five principal units preferred. | Page 1 or 2, full text width, about 2.1 inches high plus caption, roughly a quarter page. At most this one concept-generator figure. |\n| 2: Common-target budget comparison | Source-generated matplotlib point/line plot: two holdout-condition panels, fixed-audit MAE at four budgets, three pipelines, unconnected seed points plus descriptive means. | Two panels total and per row; four x categories; three method series. Seed observations use the method color, not extra legend series. | Main results, full text width, about 2.5 inches high plus caption, roughly a third page. Shared legend and y scale; each panel about 2.5 inches wide. |\n| 3: Historical-alignment control | Matplotlib paired-dot comparison: one panel per condition, three pipelines, original and permuted history, fixed-audit MAE averaged over budgets. | Two panels total and per row; three method categories; two arm series. Show recorded seed points and means. | Main results, full text width, about 2.2 inches high plus caption, roughly a quarter to a third page. Short method labels, common y scale, no cramped inset. |\n\nAll diagrams describe information flow, not research chronology. Budget on Figure 2’s x-axis is the scientific resource variable, not campaign progression. No thresholded success-rate figure is needed.\n\nCreate two compact main tables: the aggregate four-method primary/common-target comparison, and the six-row split-seed proposed-versus-ridge comparison. Do not duplicate them with redundant bar charts. Generate detailed numerical tables from evidence, not from copied plot coordinates.\n\nEvery quantitative figure must export **vector PDF and a 300-DPI PNG from source**; LaTeX uses PDF whenever available. Preserve the editable draw.io source for the overview. Raster-only full-width output must be regenerated at least 2400 pixels wide; a genuinely column-width raster in a different format requires at least 1200 pixels. Never upscale existing raster images. In the downstream figure manifest, record placement, intended width fraction, and minimum rendered font. No figures are generated in this stage.\n\n## Body Budget and Thesis-First Layout\n\nTarget approximately **8.75 body pages**, within the ICLR 2026 brief’s 8–9 pages excluding references. An eight-page body must have its last page at least 75% full. These allocations include associated floats and captions; do not add figure space a second time.\n\n| Scientific content | Approximate pages |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Title, abstract, introduction, early overview | 1.55 |\n| Related work and precise problem formulation | 1.00 |\n| Missingness-aware inference, selection, validation, fallback | 1.65 |\n| Data and evaluation protocol, baselines, target-set definitions | 1.20 |\n| Accuracy/budget comparisons, adverse split, alignment controls | 2.15 |\n| Discussion, limitations, reproducibility | 0.95 |\n| Conclusion | 0.25 |\n\nSuggested headings: Introduction; Related Work and Problem Formulation; Missingness-Aware Latent Prediction and Transfer Selection; Evaluation Protocol; Prediction Accuracy and Budget Trade-offs; Dependence on Historical Alignment; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Use extra space for a faithful conditional-inference explanation, protocol clarity, and adverse-case interpretation—not branch history or filler. Do not overfill beyond nine pages, and do not leave an obviously underfilled eight-page body.\n\nDouble-blind requirements remain hard constraints: omit author identities, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, identifying links, and internal absolute paths. Keep a separate `LLM Usage` section after references in the supplement.\n\n## Supplementary Information After References\n\nThe supplement adds auditable depth, not a hiding place for central negative evidence:\n\n1. **Complete Budget-Resolved Prediction Errors.** All recorded checkpoints, split seeds, conditions, original/permuted arms, evaluated pipelines, historical means, and queried-score averages, for both error definitions. Exclude replay from averages. Grounding: `final.evidence.episodes` and the corresponding final metric IDs.\n2. **Aggregation and Common-Target Comparisons.** Exact weights, seed-level paired deltas, and policy-specific versus unchanged-target views. Grounding: `final.evidence.episodes`; implementation: `task/evaluate_final.py`.\n3. **Observed-Data Inference and Historical Model Choice.** Faithful EM/conditioning equations, implemented candidate grids, regularization, bounded median, transfer heuristics, historical-only fixed-target validation, and fallback. The source’s `max(0.0001, 0.5 paired SE)` margin is a selection heuristic, not a significance test. Grounding is the selected `src/covariance.py`, `src/mixture.py`, and `src/METHOD.md`, not unverified internal performance. These files lack catalogued paper-data source IDs; use explicit paths rather than inventing IDs.\n4. **Frozen Data, Score Contexts, and Reproduction.** Pinned source revision, normalization, task-context keys, resource checksums, natural missingness, name-proxy families, mask-defined availability, seeds, dependency versions, and existing commands/interface. Sources: frozen resources, task contract, final manifest, `final.evidence.episodes`, and qualification provenance. Separate recorded evaluator timing from unmeasured method-only runtime or actual evaluation-cost savings. Anonymize public paths.\n5. **Permutation and Reset Controls.** Complete recorded control tables and deterministic replay; distinguish protocol checks from predictive evidence. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, permutation metric IDs, and `final.qualification.*`.\n6. **LLM Usage.** Disclose agent roles supported by implementation/status documents and append-only logs; add later drafting/figure assistance only if it occurs. Do not claim human validation, unread literature review, unrun experiments, or an undocumented model identity. No fabricated scientific source IDs are needed for this process disclosure.\n\nThe writer may receive a later authoritative analysis ranking; route appendix-ranked material here without retroactively altering this strategy. No absent robustness study or posthoc ranking is presumed complete.\n\n## Limitations That Must Stay Visible\n\n- Only pinned English embedding panels are evaluated, not generative LLMs, multilingual tasks, every upstream model, or every MTEB benchmark.\n- Naturally missing target scores have no ground truth here. Retrospective query availability is supplied from each target row’s recorded mask with audit columns removed. This does not establish prospective availability or arbitrary missing-not-at-random recovery.\n- Name-derived family proxies and frozen-data content checks do not guarantee complete architectural/lineage independence, population-level generalization, or absence of all contamination.\n- Cross-pipeline comparisons do not identify which component helps. Actual mixture choices, acquisition adaptation, fallback frequency, uncertainty calibration, cost-weighted selection, missingness sensitivity, and additional-dataset replication are not externally established.\n- Query count is not measured compute or monetary savings; real benchmarks have unequal costs. No universal sufficient budget or accepted-error threshold is demonstrated.\n- The adverse split, small and dependent split-seed set, target-set dependence, and incomplete mechanism attribution belong in the main discussion even though aggregate errors favor the procedure.\n\n## Literature and Writing Guardrails\n\n`references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` exist. At inspection they contain a retrieved method-oriented pool, including `Ghahramani1996TheEA`; essential MTEB and several explicitly allowed starter titles are not in that pool. Retrieval metadata is not evidence of reading a source’s scientific conclusions. Before final drafting, verify a small relevant subset of permitted original sources and safely retrieve missing explicitly allowed starter works. Avoid broad discovery, citation chasing, and all prohibited work or discussion of it. Do not infer or search for the prohibited paper’s identity. No network retrieval was performed for this strategy.\n\nDo not pad related work with irrelevant retrieval hits, assert novelty priority from an incomplete bibliography, or claim comparisons to unrun published methods. Position the contribution as a concrete, reproducible integration and bounded empirical finding unless stronger grounding is supplied.\n\nForbidden headings include Search Progression, Campaign Timeline, Milestones, First Valid Result, Phase One / Phase Two, Iteration History, Experiment A / Experiment B, From Gaussian to Mixture to Quadrature, Architecture Improvements, and State-of-the-Art Benchmark Compression.\n\nForbidden phrases include “we then tried,” “subsequent improvements,” “first valid result,” “next-best,” “later variants,” “the winning idea,” “the latest variant proves,” “all families improve,” “eight benchmarks are sufficient,” “statistically significant improvement,” “the mixture caused the gain,” “quadrature improves prediction,” “calibrated uncertainty,” and “state of the art.” Internal field/agent names listed in the JSON are prohibited in visible manuscript text; the path and source-ID discussion here is private writer guidance, not recommended paper language.\n\n## Handoff and Stage Boundaries\n\nThe accompanying JSON mirrors this memo and provides per-figure aggregation, source paths/IDs, and export requirements. The writer must use final metric sources for measured headline claims and record exact episode pointers for derived plots and tables. Static implementation details do not become measured claims through a citation to external episode records.\n\nOnly strategy consistency checks are appropriate now. Do not create `paper.tex` or `claims_grounding.json`, run new experiments, generate final figures, or invoke final-paper validation at this stage.\n'\"'''\nmemo = memo.replace('ADVERSE_PRIMARY',str(adverse_case['losses'][0])).replace('ADVERSE_RIDGE_AUDIT',str(adverse_case['baseline_audit'][1])).replace('ADVERSE_RIDGE',str(adverse_case['losses'][4])).replace('ADVERSE_AUDIT',str(adverse_case['audit_mae']))\nPath('paper_strategy.md').write_text(memo)\nprint('Wrote paper_strategy.json and paper_strategy.md')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
